-\subsection{Structure at Different Representational Levels}
+\subsection{Granular-Ball Organization and Hypergraph Learning}
+Granular-ball computing supplies a representation of groups at different
+scales \citep{xia2019granularball}; it is more specific than granular
+computing in general. SAGHL uses graph topology to construct hierarchical
+hyperedges and couples them to learned representations \citep{ni2026saghl}.
+The MGHRL preprint partitions graph nodes using shortest-path assignments
+and combines multiple hypergraph-network columns \citep{zhao2026mghrl}.
+Granular-ball divergence and fuzzy rough hypergraphs also support feature
+selection \citep{li2026gbfrh}. These precedents cover the general combination
+of granular balls and hypergraphs. We do not claim to originate it.
+
+Our evaluated implementation instead partitions sentence embeddings and
+uses the resulting groups for query-conditioned, inference-time selection.
+Its cosine geometry, static seed-relative scores and answer-quality endpoint
+differ from graph-topological learning or feature discrimination. These
+differences define an application and evaluation question; they do not by
+themselves establish a superior construction. In particular, shortest-path
+metric arguments cannot be transferred to one-minus-cosine dissimilarity,
+and classification improvements do not imply QA evidence utility.
+
